; =====================================================================
; cddl-prelude-number.cddl
;
; VERBATIM.  This is the whole of the formal grammar notation that
; draft-mcnally-deterministic-cbor-18 contains: one CDDL rule, in
; Section 3 ("CDDL support, Declarative Tag"), reproduced below
; byte-for-byte from line 417 of the cached source text, its three
; leading spaces included.
;
; Read the provenance carefully, because it is easy to over-cite.  The
; rule is NOT dCBOR's own definition of anything.  Section 3 quotes it
; from somewhere else, in these words:
;
;     "The CDDL Standard Prelude as defined in [RFC8610] Appendix D
;      defines:"
;
; So the authority for the rule is RFC 8610 Appendix D; dCBOR reproduces
; it only to make the point that follows it:
;
;     "This type encompasses the full space of CBOR numeric values
;      representable by CBOR major types 0, 1, and 7.  Therefore dCBOR
;      applications can use number to specify fields with numeric
;      values, and dCBOR's Numeric Reduction ensures that these values
;      are encoded deterministically."
;
; That is the only thing this record may cite the rule for: that a dCBOR
; application writing CDDL need not choose between an integer type and a
; float type for a numeric field, because Numeric Reduction (Section 2.5)
; settles the encoding.  It is an argument about how to *use* existing
; CDDL, not a grammar for dCBOR.  dCBOR ships no CDDL profile of itself;
; see dcbor-profile.derived.cddl, which is this library's, not the
; source's.
;
; TOOLING NOTE.  Do not compile this file together with the CDDL prelude:
; the prelude already contains this rule, and a second definition of a
; prelude name is a redefinition error in RFC 8610 tools.  The file is a
; citation held under version control, not a schema to feed a validator.
; dcbor-profile.derived.cddl deliberately defines no prelude name.
;
; Section 3 also adds the two control operators .dcbor and .dcborseq
; (registered in Section 6, Table 2), but states them in prose only --
; "exactly like .cbor and .cborseq as defined in [RFC8610] except that
; they also require the encoded data item(s) to conform to dCBOR" -- so
; there is no formal text for them to reproduce here.
; =====================================================================

; ---- BEGIN VERBATIM (draft-mcnally-deterministic-cbor-18, Section 3) ----
   number = int / float
; ---- END VERBATIM ----
